    "body": "Copying my remarks from a mailing list thread. TL;DR Document supernova's RT memory requirements and be done with it. It isn't worth mucking about with the design (high risk, low reward).\r\n\r\n--\r\n\r\nI think it's a documentation issue.\r\n\r\nThe behavior involves \"wire buffers.\" Audio rate inputs and outputs need to store an entire control block's worth of samples. These are the wire buffers.\r\n\r\nscsynth can only ever process exactly one Synth node at a time. So, it's sufficient to allocate a fixed number of wire buffers and reuse them for every node: `s.options.numWireBufs` (default 64). Note that the default is not a limit on the number of UGens. scsynth reuses wire buffers for chains of units: Saw --> RLPF --> Out only needs one wire buffer, but there are three units and two connections.\r\n\r\nSupernova can process multiple Synth nodes at one time, so it isn't safe for it to reuse a fixed pool of wire buffers. Supernova's approach is to allocate wire buffers per synth, and it allocates them from the real-time memory pool (`memSize`).\r\n\r\nI suppose in theory it might be possible to pre-allocate wire buffers per DSP thread, because supernova (as far as I know) can't process more synths simultaneously than it has DSP threads. But I would hesitate to go into supernova's internals and start messing around with it. Tim designed it this way, and I don't think he would do it casually. There's probably a good reason for this design.\r\n\r\nWhich means, in short, that we should document supernova's requirement for a larger real-time memory pool.",
